From: corruptmods <13516012+corruptmods@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: stamp and enqueue host-mode frames at fixed
 points of the refresh

On tvOS a 23.976 fps film on a 23.976 Hz mode, Dolby passed through, can
stutter hard while every mpv counter stays at zero: no drops, avsync ~0,
the layer timebase on the host clock. The display layer's own count
(AVVideoPerformanceMetrics.numberOfDroppedFrames) shows it: 10 to 25% of
frames dropped, in stretches that depend on where in the refresh mpv's
schedule falls, never elsewhere.

Host mode stamps each frame with mpv's planned display time (frame->pts),
which follows the audio clock and the container's 1 ms timestamp rounding,
and the VO enqueues each frame about 119 ms before that time. Field logs
from two Apple TVs, with the display link's refresh instants as the
reference:

- Apple TV 4K (1st generation), stamps as planned: drops while planned
  times sat 21 to 36 ms past the instant, almost none from 0 to 20. With
  every stamp 31.3 ms past the instant (this patch's first form), none
  with planned times at 22 ms past it, 7% with them at 28 ms.
- Apple TV 4K (2nd generation), every stamp 31.3 ms past the instant:
  the audio clock ran 14 ppm off the 41.709 ms refresh, the planned
  times swept the refresh every 49 minutes, and the layer dropped frames
  for the ~6 minutes of each sweep in which they sat from ~7 ms before
  the instant to ~2 ms after it (17 minutes into a film, and again after
  a seek re-rolled the phase), none elsewhere.

So the stamp's place in the refresh does not decide it, and the first
form's reasoning (a stamp on a refresh boundary, sent either side of it by
the rounding) was wrong: its stamps never moved, and the second TV still
dropped frames. What fits both logs, as a hypothesis (the layer's
internals are not documented), is that an enqueue landing in the ~10 ms
before the display takes its next frame costs a frame: 30 to 40 ms past
the instant on the first TV, 40 ms to 8 ms on the second. Enqueues from
~9 to ~27 ms past it were clean on both.

The VO now keeps both at fixed points of the refresh, from a CADisplayLink
on the main run loop whose refresh instants it reads without waiting:

- the stamp: each frame's refresh is picked with hysteresis (a lock on
  the smoothed offset of planned times from their refreshes), and the
  frame is stamped a fixed share of a refresh before it
  (--avfoundation-vsync-snap-lead, 0.25), whatever the lock;
- the enqueue: flip_page waits for the next instant a fixed share of a
  refresh past the link's (--avfoundation-vsync-snap-pace-phase, 0.42:
  17.5 ms at 23.976 Hz, the middle of the clean range), unless that would
  leave the frame under 40 ms before its stamp. The wait is under one
  refresh and changes nothing on screen.

Where the audio clock and the refresh drift, a whole frame of drift must
be absorbed somewhere. HDMI does not lock the two (audio travels with
N/CTS against the TMDS clock, which lets them differ), and the field logs
measure it against the host clock: on the 2nd generation the audio (the
item timebase, which follows the audio device) ran +0.3 +-0.4 ppm, the
refresh 14 ppm slow (two sweeps, 13.96 and 13.98 ppm), so a frame every
49 minutes; on the 1st generation audio, refresh and host were within
0.5 ppm and nothing drifted.

- Decoded (PCM) audio: the picture never steps. At one frame per refresh
  the VO asks the core (vo_set_audio_speed_hint(), new) to play the sound
  that much faster or slower; player/video.c applies it as mpv's
  display-resample does (speed_factor_a and _v, pure resampling through
  swresample's compensation), never to passthrough and only in the
  default audio-synced mode, and tells the VO whether it did. A PI servo
  on the picture's offset from its sound sets the speed (it holds the
  offset at 0, +-2 ms in tests), capped at
  --avfoundation-vsync-snap-resample-ppm (500); a mismatch that keeps it
  at the cap for a minute (24 fps on a 23.976 Hz mode, 1001 ppm) is left
  to the steps instead.
- Passthrough (and decoded audio past the cap): the step is hidden;
  passthrough itself is never turned off or resampled. The picture may
  drift within a lip-sync range of 60 ms early to 25 ms late against its
  sound (--avfoundation-vsync-snap-sound-late-ms, -sound-early-ms; ITU-R
  BT.1359 detects sound 45 ms early and 125 ms late; EBU R37 allows 40
  early and 60 late; up to 90 late may be set), so the step can be taken
  anywhere in a stretch of 43 ms of drift (52 minutes at 14 ppm; 87 at
  90 ms). From the middle of that stretch it is taken at a hard cut or
  the frame after a black or uniform one (a fade through black), or a
  still picture; from three quarters on at a frame changing no more than
  the calmest lately; only an unreadable frame leaves it to the end. A
  skip drops the frame before (scored by the change from two frames back
  to this one), a repeat holds it (scored by the change to this one).
  Scores are the 75th percentile of the per-cell change over a 32x18 luma
  grid: how much of the picture moves, so a still camera with one person
  moving ranks far above a slow even pan, where a dropped frame is the
  classic visible judder. Every format mpv gives this VO is sampled
  (VideoToolbox's 8- and 10-bit biplanar 4:2:0/4:2:2/4:4:4, packed 4:2:2,
  BGRA; NV12, P010, yuv420p, -p10, -p12 from software decoding), 4.6 us a
  4K frame on an M1. A step only follows a measured drift (10 s spans
  once the lock settles, three agreeing or one over 50 ppm), so locked
  clocks never step. At a fresh lock (seek, unpause) at one frame per
  refresh the picture starts in the middle of the range. Cadences (23.976
  fps on 60 Hz, a 2,3 slip every 17 s) hide their slips the same way.

The refresh clock keeps its grid across a stopped link (background, a
sleeping display): a gap that comes back on the grid keeps the run, one at
another phase starts a new run but keeps the measured period until the new
one has its own, and a mode change (the TV going to 60 Hz and back) starts
over; the lock is taken again once each time, and the resampling servo
starts over on another mode.

The enqueue's pacing now waits with the OSD and video samples already
made, and aims early by the measured lateness of the video enqueue after
it (the OSD enqueue between them, a few ms), so the enqueue lands on its
point (build 44 put it at 21.9 ms for 17.5).

--avfoundation-vsync-snap-pace=auto (the default) also reads the layer's
counts once a second and moves the enqueue point on by a quarter refresh
after two 5 s windows in a row with drops (3 or more, and 2%), for a
display whose timing differs from the two measured; yes keeps the point,
no enqueues on mpv's schedule as before. A seek, an unpause, a refresh
rate change or refreshes that moved take a new lock; no refresh clock
keeps the planned time and mpv's schedule.

iOS/iPadOS and macOS run media mode: there the host time at which the
timebase reaches the PTS is snapped and mapped back, from a passive 15 Hz
link (iOS) or the hosting NSView's display link (macOS 14+), and nothing
is paced. A 120 Hz iPad Pro dropped about a quarter of its frames with
the snap on (cause unknown), so the default stays off outside tvOS.

--avfoundation-vsync-snap=yes|no|measure (yes on tvOS, no elsewhere),
-snap-lead (0.25), -snap-pace=no|yes|auto (auto), -snap-pace-phase
(0.42), -snap-resample-ppm (500, 0 = never resample), -snap-sound-late-ms
(60) and -snap-sound-early-ms (25). Every 5 s at -v the VO logs three lines (each under 200
characters, which is what Pluet's field log keeps of a line), and a
fourth on the steps, e.g.:

  vsync-snap on: 120 frames/5.0s (0 no refresh), refresh 41.709 ms (link
  41.709), lock +2.25 ms, layer -0.024 ms, moved=0, shift avg +2.22 max
  2.74 ms, relocks=0 reslots=0
  vsync-snap phase (ms past refresh): planned 39.49 [-0.51 +0.52], stamp
  31.28 [+0.00 +0.00], enqueue 17.52 [-0.01 +0.40], refreshes 1:119 2:0
  0:0, uneven A=0 B=0 S=0
  vsync-snap pace auto 0.42: lead min 56.1 avg 99.0 max 131.0 ms, pace
  wait avg 20.3 max 41.0, ready wait avg 0.0 max 0.0, layer shown 120
  dropped 0, steps=0
  vsync-snap sync: av -14.8..-13.9 ms, drift +14.0 ppm, resample +0.0
  ppm off; steps cut 1 black 0 still 0 low 0 forced 0, last cut 40.00 at
  +6.1 ms; sampled 120 cuts 2 flat 0

"lock" is the shown refresh minus the planned time (smoothed), "moved"
counts frames on another refresh than the first at or after their planned
time, "shift" is the picture's offset from its planned time; the phase
line gives where the planned times, the stamps and the enqueues fell
past the link's refresh instant (mean [spread]), and the refreshes the
frames went to (one frame each, two in one, none); "uneven" counts
frames a refresh early or late against the one before (A: stamped as
planned on the first refresh at or after, B: on the nearest, S: as
snapped). The pace line gives each frame's lead before its stamp at the
enqueue, the pacing and readiness waits, the layer's own counts for the
window, and how often auto moved the point; the sync line the picture's
offset from its sound over the window (+: picture late), the measured
drift, the resampling asked (and whether the core applies it), the steps
since the file opened by where they were hidden, the last one's kind,
score and offset, and how many frames were sampled, cuts, flat frames
and frames that could not be read.

scripts/test_avf_vsync_snap.sh compiles the snap and the pacing from the
final Apple series and drives them with synthetic clocks: every phase at
23.976 on 23.976 Hz, drift, the 60/50 Hz cadences, 24 fps on 120 and 48
Hz, refresh-rate changes mid-play, a window moved to another display, a
seek, no refresh clock, 55 minutes of the second TV's drift (one frame
per refresh but for one clean step, stamps at one point, enqueues held at
one point and out of both TVs' late ranges), the auto rule; three hours
at 14 ppm, passthrough, of a film, a pan with no cut, a static shot,
talking heads, fades, unreadable frames and slow pans alternating with a
still camera (4 steps each, at cuts, blacks, stills, the pan's calmest
frames, never in a pan when a still camera comes; forced only when
unreadable), the 60 and 90 ms ranges, a fast refresh (repeats at cuts),
locked clocks (none); decoded audio at 14 and +-300 ppm (no step) and
1001 ppm (the steps take over, at cuts); 23.976 on 60 Hz (every slip at a
cut); a pause with the display waking at another phase; the refresh clock
through gaps and mode changes; a link gap, 60 Hz and back and a seek
while paused in one run; pacing at a start; and the classifiers.


diff --git a/player/video.c b/player/video.c
index 4d365a3..4c33302 100644
--- a/player/video.c
+++ b/player/video.c
@@ -974,6 +974,23 @@ static void schedule_frame(struct MPContext *mpctx, struct vo_frame *frame)
             mpctx->speed_factor_v = 1.0;
             update_playback_speed(mpctx);
         }
+        // A VO showing one frame per refresh (vo_avfoundation's vsync snap)
+        // may ask for the sound to follow the display instead: decoded audio
+        // is resampled by that much, and video timed to match, so the
+        // picture needs no repeat or skip. Never for passthrough (it cannot
+        // be resampled), and only in the default audio-synced mode.
+        double hint = vo_get_audio_speed_hint(mpctx->video_out);
+        bool apply = hint > 0.99 && hint < 1.01 && mpctx->ao_chain &&
+                     !mpctx->ao_chain->spdif_passthrough &&
+                     mpctx->audio_status == STATUS_PLAYING &&
+                     mpctx->video_out->opts->video_sync == VS_DEFAULT;
+        double want = apply ? hint : 1.0;
+        if (want != mpctx->speed_factor_a || want != mpctx->speed_factor_v) {
+            mpctx->speed_factor_a = want;
+            mpctx->speed_factor_v = want;
+            update_playback_speed(mpctx);
+        }
+        vo_set_audio_speed_applied(mpctx->video_out, apply);
 
         update_av_diff(mpctx, mpctx->time_frame > 0 ?
             mpctx->time_frame * mpctx->video_speed : 0);
diff --git a/video/out/vo.c b/video/out/vo.c
index b109ee1..664fd26 100644
--- a/video/out/vo.c
+++ b/video/out/vo.c
@@ -163,6 +163,8 @@ struct vo_internal {
 
     int64_t flip_queue_offset; // queue flip events at most this much in advance
     int64_t queue_slack;       // vo_set_queue_slack()
+    double audio_speed_hint;   // vo_set_audio_speed_hint(), 0: none
+    bool audio_speed_applied;  // vo_set_audio_speed_applied()
     int64_t timing_offset;     // same (but from options; not VO configured)
 
     int64_t delayed_count;
@@ -1359,6 +1361,44 @@ void vo_set_queue_params(struct vo *vo, int64_t offset_ns, int num_req_frames)
     mp_mutex_unlock(&in->lock);
 }
 
+// A VO that shows one frame per display refresh may ask for the audio to be
+// played this much faster (or slower, under 1) instead of the picture being
+// stepped: the core applies it by resampling decoded audio, never to
+// passthrough, and says whether it did (vo_get_audio_speed_applied()).
+void vo_set_audio_speed_hint(struct vo *vo, double speed)
+{
+    struct vo_internal *in = vo->in;
+    mp_mutex_lock(&in->lock);
+    in->audio_speed_hint = speed;
+    mp_mutex_unlock(&in->lock);
+}
+
+double vo_get_audio_speed_hint(struct vo *vo)
+{
+    struct vo_internal *in = vo->in;
+    mp_mutex_lock(&in->lock);
+    double r = in->audio_speed_hint;
+    mp_mutex_unlock(&in->lock);
+    return r;
+}
+
+void vo_set_audio_speed_applied(struct vo *vo, bool applied)
+{
+    struct vo_internal *in = vo->in;
+    mp_mutex_lock(&in->lock);
+    in->audio_speed_applied = applied;
+    mp_mutex_unlock(&in->lock);
+}
+
+bool vo_get_audio_speed_applied(struct vo *vo)
+{
+    struct vo_internal *in = vo->in;
+    mp_mutex_lock(&in->lock);
+    bool r = in->audio_speed_applied;
+    mp_mutex_unlock(&in->lock);
+    return r;
+}
+
 void vo_set_queue_slack(struct vo *vo, int64_t slack_ns)
 {
     struct vo_internal *in = vo->in;
